{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Teaching in Tech-Rich Classrooms With TeachGen@i","title":"Tech-Rich Classroom Routines","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting tech-rich classroom routines to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"01","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i","cat531_supplement_tech_rich_classroom_routines","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/01/08"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_2_1_tech_rich_classroom_routines_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_2_1_tech_rich_classroom_routines_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_2_1_tech_rich_classroom_routines_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_2_1_tech_rich_classroom_routines_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Tech-Rich Classroom Routines\n\n![Tech-Rich Classroom Routines textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/02-01-tech-rich-classroom-routines.png)\n\n<youtube-embed id=\"yMQiHJsePOM\" title=\"What Is The TPACK Model?\" caption=\"Use this as a quick lens before revising the classroom routine: the tool is only useful when it fits the teaching move and the content goal.\"></youtube-embed>\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA classroom routine is a repeated, low-decision sequence (the warm-up, the exit slip, the turn-and-talk, the cleanup) that runs almost automatically once students learn it. Routines matter because they conserve a teacher's scarcest resource, attention. When the procedure is automatic, the teacher can spend cognitive effort on the lesson rather than on crowd control. The risk in a tech-rich classroom is that a device or app silently rewrites the routine without anyone deciding it should. A polling app turns \"raise your hand\" into \"log in and submit\"; a learning platform turns \"I noticed Maya struggling\" into \"the dashboard flagged a red cell.\" Each substitution changes who has access, what counts as participation, and where student data goes.\n\nThis section teaches you to treat a tech-rich routine as a decision you make on purpose, not a default the tool installs for you. The artifact you build, a Design Tension Studio map, forces that decision into the open: it names the value that the technology helps (often speed, reach, or instant feedback) and the value it threatens (often access, privacy, or a slower kind of thinking), then records the compromise you negotiated and why.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nMr. Alvarez runs a daily five-minute math warm-up. For years it was a printed problem on the board; students worked it silently while he took attendance. A vendor demo convinced his department to adopt a live-polling app: students answer on phones, the screen shows a real-time bar chart, and \"engagement\" jumps because everyone is tapping. Three weeks in, he notices that the four students who never bring charged phones now sit out the warm-up entirely, and a fifth copies a neighbor's screen to avoid looking unprepared. The tool did not just speed up his routine; it quietly converted a universally accessible task into one gated by hardware. The question for the map is not \"is polling good?\" but \"which value did this routine trade away, and was that trade defensible for these students?\"\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA tech-rich routine should be analyzed as a swap, not an upgrade. Every time a tool enters a routine, it trades one set of properties for another, and a defensible teacher names both sides of the trade before keeping the tool. Three properties of a routine almost always shift when technology enters it.\n\n| Routine property | What the analog version protects | What the digital version often trades it for |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Access | Anyone present can participate (paper, voice, hands) | Participation now requires a device, login, or bandwidth |\n| Data exposure | Responses stay in the room and disappear | Responses are stored, scored, and sometimes sold or profiled |\n| Pace of thinking | Time to reason before answering | Speed and instant scoring, which can reward guessing |\n\nThe work of this section is to run any candidate routine through this swap analysis. TPACK supplies the discipline: a tool is only justified when its technological affordance genuinely serves both the pedagogical move (here, a quick diagnostic) and the content (the specific math idea), rather than serving novelty. Critical edtech studies supplies the suspicion: ask who is burdened by the swap and whose data is now in motion. A routine passes when you can state the learning purpose, the value you protected, and the value you knowingly traded with eyes open.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Mr. Alvarez's polling app made the warm-up faster and showed a live engagement chart. Under the swap analysis, what did the digital version most clearly trade away?\" explanation=\"The bar chart counts only students who could log in. The clearest casualty is the access floor: four students without charged phones can no longer participate at all, so the routine quietly gated a once-universal task behind hardware.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Access, because participation now requires a charged device and login\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Nothing, because higher engagement numbers prove the swap was an upgrade\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Content accuracy, because the math problem itself changed\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWalk through one annotated judgment call on a real routine. A seventh-grade science teacher wants a daily warm-up routine and is deciding between two designs for the same five minutes.\n\n**Step 1. Name the learning move.** The warm-up should surface the most common error from yesterday so the teacher can open with a targeted re-teach. That is the purpose the routine must serve.\n\n**Step 2. Lay out the value tension on the map.** Speed of feedback (a live polling app that scores instantly) competes with access (three students have unreliable home logins and arrive having missed the prompt). Naming this tension is the work; it is not a tooling preference.\n\n**Step 3. Make the before/after decision.**\n\n> **Before (tool-first draft):** \"Students answer a TeachGen@i poll the night before; the dashboard ranks responses, and we open class with the leaderboard.\" A mentor reading this sees engagement language and a home-access assumption, but no fallback and no learning move tied to the data.\n\n> **After (defensible revision):** \"Students answer the same prompt in the first ninety seconds of class on any device or on a printed half-sheet. I read the three most common wrong answers off the dashboard, then re-teach that one error for three minutes. Decision recorded on the map: I rejected the home-login version because it excluded the three students with unreliable access and pushed the diagnostic outside the room where I cannot act on it.\"\n\n**Step 4. Leave the evidence.** The after version names the tension (feedback speed vs. access), the negotiated decision (in-class entry with a non-digital fallback), and the rejected option with its reason. A mentor can now audit the call instead of taking \"it was engaging\" on trust.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nFor a routine, protect access before you optimize speed. Concretely: a routine is defensible only if every student physically present can complete it without owning, charging, or logging into a personal device. If the digital version cannot meet that floor, keep the digital version as an enhancement layered on top of an analog floor (the printed half-sheet, the spoken answer), never as the only door in.\n\nRun a candidate routine through three checks before you commit it:\n\n1. **Access floor:** Can a student with no working device still participate fully today?\n2. **Data check:** What student information leaves the room, who stores it, and is that exposure proportional to the learning gain?\n3. **Pace check:** Does the tool give students time to reason, or does its scoring clock reward fast guessing over thinking?\n\nA routine that fails the access floor is not ready, no matter how engaging the dashboard looks.\n\nWalk a live version of Mr. Alvarez's decision and watch where each choice lands on the swap analysis.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The warm-up polling decision\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"Three weeks into the polling-app warm-up, four students without charged phones sit out and one copies a neighbor. The vendor chart still shows high engagement. What is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep the app as the only door; engagement numbers look great\",\"next\":\"only_door\",\"feedback\":\"You read the engagement chart as evidence of learning. It only shows that students who could log in did.\"},{\"label\":\"Add a printed half-sheet so any student present can answer, app optional\",\"next\":\"floor\",\"feedback\":\"You restored the access floor: every student present can now complete the warm-up without a device.\"},{\"label\":\"Buy a class set of loaner phones to close the gap\",\"next\":\"loaner\",\"feedback\":\"A hardware fix can help, but it leaves the routine dependent on devices working and charged every day.\"}]},\"only_door\":{\"prompt\":\"Next week the same four students are still excluded and a parent asks why their child has zero warm-up scores. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Admit the routine failed the access floor and add an analog fallback\",\"next\":\"floor\",\"feedback\":\"You arrived at the access floor the hard way. Naming the swap up front would have prevented the harm.\"},{\"label\":\"Tell the parent the child should charge the phone\",\"next\":\"blame\",\"feedback\":\"This shifts a design flaw onto the student and hides the trade the tool installed.\"}]},\"floor\":{\"prompt\":\"The half-sheet works. Should you keep the dashboard at all?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep aggregation for the same-day diagnostic, turn off the public leaderboard\",\"next\":\"defensible\",\"feedback\":\"You kept the genuine gain (fast aggregate read) and refused the default that exposed and ranked students.\"},{\"label\":\"Turn the tool off entirely to be safe\",\"next\":\"defensible\",\"feedback\":\"Defensible, though you can usually layer the digital enhancement on top of the analog floor rather than dropping it.\"}]},\"loaner\":{\"prompt\":\"Two loaners are dead on Tuesday. Two students are excluded again. What does this reveal?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Hardware alone is not an access floor; layer an analog option underneath\",\"next\":\"floor\",\"feedback\":\"Right. A device-dependent fix can fail any day; the floor must work with no working device.\"}]},\"blame\":{\"prompt\":\"This records who was burdened by the swap. The defensible move is still to restore the floor.\",\"feedback\":\"A routine that fails the access floor is not ready, no matter how engaging the dashboard looks.\",\"choices\":[]},\"defensible\":{\"prompt\":\"You can now state the learning purpose, the value you protected (access), and the value you traded with eyes open. The map records one accepted feature and one refused default.\",\"feedback\":\"This is a defensible tech-rich routine: analog floor first, digital enhancement layered on, one default refused with a reason.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nThe most common error is to read engagement metrics (taps, logins, time-on-task) as evidence of learning. A bar chart that updates in real time feels like proof, but it only shows that students who could log in did. It is silent about the students it excluded and about whether anyone reasoned. A second misreading is to treat the tool's default settings as neutral. A polling app that defaults to a public leaderboard has made a pedagogical choice (competition, exposure) on your behalf; if you keep that default, you own that choice. The map should record which defaults you accepted and which you overrode.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the swap analysis to one concrete routine: the exit ticket. The analog exit ticket is a sticky note answered on the way out; it is universal, private, and disappears after the teacher reads it. Suppose a platform offers a digital exit ticket that auto-scores responses and builds a per-student trend line. Name the swap: you gain instant aggregation and a longitudinal record, and you trade away the access floor (students without a device cannot answer) and the privacy floor (responses now persist in a vendor system tied to student names).\n\nA defensible revision keeps the gain without paying the full price. You let students answer on a device or on paper, you collect the paper answers into the same tally by hand, and you turn off the per-student trend line because a daily error pattern does not justify a permanent profile. The map records one accepted feature (aggregation), one rejected feature (the persistent profile), and the reason: the learning purpose was a same-day diagnostic, not a surveillance history.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: in the left column write the routine and the value the tool advertises; in the right column write the value the tool quietly costs. For the polling warm-up, the left column says \"instant feedback, high engagement\" and the right column says \"excludes uncharged phones, rewards guessing under the clock.\" A strong reader of this section can look at any tech-rich routine and immediately ask: what is on the right column that the vendor did not mention? Your map is strong when its right column is as specific as its left.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong routine map does three things a weak one does not. First, it states the analog baseline, so a reader can see exactly what changed when the tool entered. Second, it names the access floor explicitly and shows how the final routine meets it (the printed fallback, the spoken option). Third, it records at least one tool feature you turned off or refused, with the reason, because a routine that accepts every default is a tool's design, not a teacher's. Polish (clean icons, a tidy layout) is the last layer; if the access floor is missing, no amount of polish makes the routine defensible.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the polling warm-up to a one-to-one district where every student has a charged, school-managed tablet. The access floor is now met by the hardware, so the dominant tension shifts: speed versus pace of thinking becomes the live conflict, and the data question sharpens because school-managed devices log everything by default. The routine does not copy over unchanged; the value you must protect changes from access to thinking time and data minimization. If you can re-derive the map for the new setting instead of pasting the old one, you understand routines as swaps rather than as fixed recipes.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A colleague says, 'My exit-ticket app boosts engagement, so swapping the sticky notes for it is an upgrade.' Using the swap analysis, explain in two or three sentences why higher engagement is not by itself evidence that the trade was defensible, and name the specific value you would check before keeping the app.\" answer=\"Engagement metrics only count students who could log in; they are silent about the students the device gated out and about whether anyone reasoned. The swap is an upgrade only if the routine still meets the access floor (every student present can answer without owning or charging a device) and the data exposure is proportional to the learning gain. So before keeping the app I would check the access floor and what student data now leaves the room, not the engagement number.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/02-01-tech-rich-classroom-routines-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"02.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **TPACK**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 02.01. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_tech_rich_classroom_routines\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your map states the analog baseline, names the access floor and how the routine meets it, and records at least one tool feature you refused with its reason. That swap analysis is what separates a teacher's routine from a vendor's default.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_tech_rich_classroom_routines\", \"cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- TPACK. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- AI and education datafication. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_1_tech_rich_classroom_routines_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_tech_rich_classroom_routines","stem":"A teacher wants to add a daily five-minute entry routine using a polling app. Which justification makes the routine defensible rather than tool-driven?","options":["The app shows a live bar chart, so the class will find it fun.","The poll surfaces yesterday's most common error so the teacher can open with a targeted re-teach.","Every student has a district device, so the routine will run smoothly.","The app can timestamp responses for the gradebook."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The defensible version names the learning move the routine serves (diagnosing and re-teaching a common error). The other options justify the routine by tool features or smooth operation.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_1_tech_rich_classroom_routines_tool_first_routine","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i","cat531_supplement_tech_rich_classroom_routines","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_1_tech_rich_classroom_routines_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_tech_rich_classroom_routines","stem":"After running a gamified review routine, a teacher writes in the Tension Studio map: \"Students loved it, leaderboard was packed.\" What is the strongest reason this is not yet evidence that the routine should stay?","options":["Leaderboards are never appropriate in a classroom.","Enthusiasm and on-task behavior do not show whether the targeted concept was learned.","The note is too short to count as a portfolio entry.","The teacher should have used a different app."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Engagement is necessary but not sufficient. The map needs a learning check or artifact, not just evidence of attention, to justify keeping the routine.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_1_tech_rich_classroom_routines_engagement_equals_learning","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_tech_rich_classroom_routines","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_1_tech_rich_classroom_routines_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"A teacher asks TeachGen@i to design a tech-rich opening routine. The draft assumes every student logs in from a personal device at home the night before. What is the most defensible teacher move?","options":["Adopt the routine as written because the AI optimized it for engagement.","Reject the home-login assumption and add an in-class, non-device fallback so a student without home access still completes the learning move.","Remove the routine entirely because AI cannot plan equitable routines.","Keep the assumption but tell students to use the library before school."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The teacher names who the routine excludes when access fails and builds a fallback that preserves the learning move, treating the AI draft as a proposal rather than authority.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_1_tech_rich_classroom_routines_access_assumed_uniform","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_tech_rich_classroom_routines","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_1_tech_rich_classroom_routines_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"On the Design Tension Studio map for a new tech-rich routine, which paired entry best documents a defensible decision a mentor could audit?","options":["\"Routine is engaging\" paired with \"students like the app.\"","Tension named (instructional time vs. device setup) paired with the negotiated decision (move setup to the prior afternoon so the routine reclaims four minutes of instruction).","\"Used technology\" paired with \"finished on time.\"","\"Followed the lesson plan\" paired with \"no complaints.\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A defensible map pairs a named value tension with the negotiated decision and its consequence, which is exactly what a mentor can inspect and question.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_1_tech_rich_classroom_routines_tool_first_routine","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_tech_rich_classroom_routines","evidence_based_revision"]}]}}